README.OSK -- snake as it is kept on this diskTwo changes from the source the archive carried (ORIG/ holds both filesas they came):  move.c   raw() and cook() were empty, so snake never turned the           terminal's echo off and every key it read was echoed onto the           board.  They now clear and restore sg_echo with _gs_opt and           _ss_opt.  (2026-08-27)  snake.c  main() no longer sets _UNBUF on stdout.  With it, the C           library gives stdout a one-byte buffer, and after each fflush()           -- snake flushes before every read -- the next character is           stored instead of written: that was the ESC of the next cursor           move, which then arrived after its own sequence, so moves were           printed on the board as text, and the last held ESC ate the           first letter of the shell's prompt on exit.  stdout stays           buffered; snake already flushes before each read and on exit.           stdin keeps _RBF and _UNBUF, which is what makes keys arrive one           at a time.  (2026-09-24)Built by tools/rebuild's `snake' recipe.